---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Tile Leveling Systems in 2026: Tested";
const description =
  "We set 400 square feet of porcelain with seven leveling systems. Clips versus wedges versus threaded caps, and which ones actually stop lippage.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "rubi-delta",
    name: "Rubi Delta Leveling System",
    category: "Clip and Wedge, Reusable Wedges, 1mm and 2mm Clips",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2400,
    pros: [
      "Wedges are reusable almost indefinitely, which changes the running cost dramatically",
      "Clips break cleanly at the base rather than shearing above the tile",
      "Works with tile from 3mm up to 20mm thickness",
      "Pliers apply consistent tension without crushing the wedge",
      "Widely available with predictable clip and wedge restocking",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Higher upfront cost for the pliers and starter kit",
      "Clips are single use, as with every clip-and-wedge system",
      "Overkill for small format tile that does not need leveling at all",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The system professionals reach for. Reusable wedges, clean breaks, and the most consistent results in our testing.",
    description:
      "Every clip-and-wedge system works the same way in principle, and the differences that matter show up in two places: whether the clip breaks where it is supposed to, and whether the wedges survive to be used again. Rubi Delta is the best we tested at both. The clip has a defined weak point right at the base, below the tile surface, so a firm kick along the grout line snaps it flush rather than shearing above the tile and leaving a plastic nub you have to dig out before grouting. Across roughly 400 clips we had a handful of bad breaks, which is a far better rate than the budget systems produced. The wedges are the real economic argument. They come out intact, wash clean, and go back in the bucket, which means the recurring cost of the system is clips alone. On a large job that is a meaningful difference, because wedges are the more expensive component and cheap systems chew through them. The pliers are well made and apply even tension without crushing the wedge nose, which is the usual failure on budget tools. The reservations are cost and applicability. The starter kit costs more than most, and no leveling system is worth using on small format tile that self-aligns anyway. Buy it for large format porcelain and it pays for itself on the first job.",
  },
  {
    id: "perfect-level-master",
    name: "Perfect Level Master T-Lock",
    category: "Clip and Wedge, T-Shaped Clip, 1mm to 3mm",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 5600,
    pros: [
      "T-shaped clip head spreads load across a wider area of tile",
      "Excellent price per clip in bulk quantities",
      "Multiple clip widths available for different grout joint sizes",
      "Wedges are reusable and hold up well over repeated jobs",
      "Snaps reliably at floor level with minimal cleanup",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Pliers are functional rather than refined compared to premium tools",
      "Wider clip head needs more thinset coverage to sit properly",
      "Colour coding between sizes is easy to mix up in a bucket",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Nearly the performance of premium systems at a fraction of the running cost. The sensible pick for a homeowner doing one big floor.",
    description:
      "The economics of tile leveling are unforgiving for a homeowner. A single bathroom floor might consume 300 clips, and at premium pricing the consumables can cost more than the tile trim. Perfect Level Master T-Lock solves that with genuinely competitive bulk pricing while giving up very little in performance, which is why it appears on so many one-off renovation jobs. The T-shaped clip head is the notable design difference. Instead of a narrow post pressing on a small area, the T spreads its load across a wider strip of the tile underside, which reduces the chance of a thin or brittle tile cracking at the clip. In our testing with 6mm porcelain that mattered, and we saw no clip-induced cracks. The trade-off is that the wider head needs proper thinset coverage beneath it to bed correctly, so a job with spotty coverage will not hold as well. Breaks were clean and consistent, and the wedges came back out reusable, which keeps the running cost low. The pliers are the weak point. They work and they apply adequate tension, but they feel agricultural next to the premium tools and your hand notices after a few hundred clips. Sizes are also colour coded in a way that is easy to confuse once several are loose in a bucket, so keep them separated.",
  },
  {
    id: "raimondi-rls",
    name: "Raimondi RLS Basic",
    category: "Clip and Wedge, Professional Grade, 1mm to 3mm",
    badge: "Best for Professionals",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 1900,
    pros: [
      "Built for daily commercial use with correspondingly robust tooling",
      "Very consistent breaking behaviour across hundreds of clips",
      "Floor and wall pliers available, which matters on vertical work",
      "Strong clip stem tolerates heavy large format tile without deflecting",
      "Long track record and easy consumable availability in the trade",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Premium pricing on both tools and consumables",
      "Aimed at professionals, so overspecified for a single bathroom",
      "Wall pliers are a separate purchase",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The commercial standard. If you set tile for a living, this is the system built for the volume.",
    description:
      "Professional and homeowner tile leveling look identical for the first fifty clips. The difference shows up at five hundred, when cheap pliers have loosened, wedges have deformed, and clip breaks have become unpredictable. Raimondi builds for that volume, and the RLS system is the one you find on commercial jobs for a reason. Clip consistency is the standout. Across our test the RLS clips broke at the intended point more reliably than anything else, which matters enormously when you are working across a large floor on a schedule and cannot afford to stop and chisel out failed stems before grouting. The clip stem is also stiff enough that it does not deflect under the weight of heavy large format porcelain, which is exactly where weaker clips let one edge sag. The availability of dedicated wall pliers is a genuine advantage that most systems ignore. Setting large tile vertically is where lippage is most visible and hardest to control, and a plier designed to reach into a vertical joint makes a real difference. What you are paying for is durability and consistency rather than any different principle, so a homeowner doing one floor will get very similar results from a cheaper system. For anyone tiling weekly, the tooling difference is worth the money within a month.",
  },
  {
    id: "peygran",
    name: "Peygran Tile Leveling System",
    category: "Clip and Wedge, Reusable Wedge, 1mm and 2mm",
    badge: "Best Reusable Wedges",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 3200,
    pros: [
      "Wedges are unusually durable and survive many more cycles than most",
      "Simple design with very few ways to use it wrong",
      "Good clip strength for the price point",
      "Comfortable pliers with a smooth action",
      "Sold in large bulk packs that suit whole-house jobs",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Only common clip thicknesses available, limiting unusual joint widths",
      "Breaking can leave a small ridge on very thin tile",
      "Less refined finish than premium European systems",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best wedge durability we tested. If you are tiling several rooms, the reusability adds up fast.",
    description:
      "The hidden cost of a tile leveling system is not the kit, it is the consumables, and the consumable that determines your running cost is the wedge. Clips are cheap and single use by design. Wedges are meant to be reused, and how many times they survive before the nose deforms is the number that decides what a thousand square feet actually costs you. Peygran wedges were the most durable in our test by a comfortable margin. After several hundred insertions the noses showed wear but still gripped correctly, where two of the budget systems had wedges rounding off and slipping under tension within a couple of hundred. For anyone tiling more than a single room that difference compounds quickly. The rest of the system is straightforward rather than clever. Clip strength is good for the price, the pliers have a smooth and comfortable action, and there are very few ways to use it incorrectly, which is a real virtue for a first-time user. Bulk packs make whole-house jobs economical. The limitations are range and finish. Only the common clip thicknesses are offered, so an unusual grout joint width may not be covered, and on very thin tile the break can leave a small ridge that needs a scrape before grouting. Neither is a serious problem on typical porcelain.",
  },
  {
    id: "qep-lash",
    name: "QEP LASH Tile Leveling System",
    category: "Clip and Wedge, Widely Stocked, 1/16 inch and 1/8 inch",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 8900,
    pros: [
      "Stocked in most big-box stores, so restocking mid-job is realistic",
      "Lowest entry cost of any system here",
      "Straightforward to use with no learning curve",
      "Adequate performance on standard thickness porcelain and ceramic",
      "Clip sizes stated in inches, which matches how joints are specified in the US",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Wedges wear out considerably faster than premium alternatives",
      "Higher rate of clips shearing above the tile surface",
      "Pliers are basic and tiring over a large floor",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The system you can buy at 8am on a Saturday. Fine for one room, frustrating across a whole house.",
    description:
      "There is a specific and very common scenario where the best leveling system is whichever one you can buy today: the tile is down to the last two boxes, the mortar is mixed, and you have discovered halfway across the floor that you need leveling clips. QEP LASH is stocked almost everywhere, in inch-denominated sizes that match how American grout joints are specified, and that availability is a genuine feature. Performance is adequate rather than good. On standard thickness porcelain and ceramic it holds tiles flush perfectly well, and for a single bathroom floor most people will never notice the difference between this and a premium system. The problems appear with volume. Our break failure rate — clips shearing above the tile rather than at the base — was noticeably higher than the premium systems, which means more time on your knees with a chisel before you can grout. Wedges also wore out faster, so the running cost advantage narrows on a big job. The pliers are basic, with a stiffer action that becomes tiring across several hundred clips. None of that matters much for one room. Across a whole house it becomes the reason people upgrade. Buy this for a small job or an emergency restock, and buy something better if you are tiling more than a couple of hundred square feet.",
  },
  {
    id: "spin-doctor",
    name: "Spin Doctor Tile Leveling System",
    category: "Threaded Cap, Reusable Caps, Adjustable Tension",
    badge: "Best Threaded System",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 2700,
    pros: [
      "Threaded caps are fully reusable, so consumables are stems only",
      "Tension is adjustable and repeatable rather than dependent on plier feel",
      "No pliers required, which suits people with limited hand strength",
      "Caps can be backed off and readjusted before the mortar sets",
      "Very effective on heavy large format tile",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Slower to install than wedges on a large floor",
      "Caps must be removed before the mortar fully cures or they seize",
      "Stems still snap off and require cleanup like any clip",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pick if hand strength is a limitation or you want repeatable, adjustable tension rather than plier feel.",
    description:
      "Clip-and-wedge systems rely on the installer squeezing a plier hard enough, and how hard is enough is a matter of feel that takes a floor or two to learn. Threaded systems replace that judgement with a screw. You place the stem, thread a cap down onto it, and tighten until the tiles pull flush, which is repeatable, adjustable, and requires almost no grip strength. For anyone with arthritis, a weak hand, or simply no desire to squeeze a plier three hundred times, that is a decisive advantage. The adjustability is the second real benefit. With a wedge, tension is set once and modifying it means pulling the wedge and starting over. With a threaded cap you can back it off, nudge a tile, and retighten while the mortar is still open, which is genuinely useful when you are working alone across large format tile and one corner has drifted. The caps are fully reusable, so your only consumable is the stem. The costs are speed and timing. Threading a cap takes longer than driving a wedge, and across a big floor that adds up to real hours. More importantly, the caps have to come off before the mortar fully cures or they bond in place, which makes this a poor choice if you might have to walk away mid-job.",
  },
  {
    id: "tuscan-leveling",
    name: "Tuscan SeamClip Leveling System",
    category: "Strap and Cap, Large Format Specialist, Up to 24 inch Tile",
    badge: "Best for Large Format",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1400,
    pros: [
      "Designed specifically around large format and gauged porcelain panels",
      "Very strong pull-down force keeps heavy tile edges genuinely flush",
      "Strap design distributes force well across long tile edges",
      "Purpose-built tensioning tool gives consistent results",
      "Handles the tile sizes where lippage is most visible",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, and clearly aimed at the professional market",
      "Unnecessary for tile under about 12 inches",
      "Requires its own dedicated tool, which is another purchase",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Built for the tile sizes that defeat ordinary clips. Overkill below 12 inches, close to essential above 24.",
    description:
      "Lippage tolerance does not scale with tile size, which is the fact that catches people out. Industry guidance allows a fixed maximum height difference between adjacent tiles regardless of how big they are, but a large tile is far more likely to be slightly bowed across its length, and any bow has to be pulled flat at the edges. That is a different job from simply holding two small tiles level, and it needs more force than a standard clip provides. Tuscan is built for exactly that. The strap and cap arrangement generates a strong, well distributed pull-down along the tile edge rather than a point load, which is what large format porcelain needs to sit flush. On 24 inch tile and gauged panels it was clearly the most effective system we tested, holding edges flat where standard clips allowed a visible rise. The dedicated tensioning tool gives repeatable results without guesswork. The case against it is straightforward: cost and scope. It is priced for professionals, it needs its own tool, and on 12 inch tile it does nothing a cheap clip system would not do just as well. If your job is large format, it is worth the money. If it is not, spend the difference on better thinset and a longer straightedge.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Tile Leveling System",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-tile-leveling-system/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best tile leveling system?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Rubi Delta is the best all-round choice, with the cleanest clip breaks and the most durable reusable wedges. Perfect Level Master T-Lock offers nearly the same performance at a much lower running cost, and Tuscan is the specialist pick for large format tile above 24 inches.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I really need a tile leveling system?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not for small format tile or mosaics, which largely self-align. The case grows with tile size and becomes compelling above about 12 inches, because large tile is more likely to be slightly bowed and any height difference between edges is far more visible on a long joint.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size tile leveling clips do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The clip width sets your grout joint, so match it to the joint you want: 2mm for rectified porcelain, 3mm or more for pressed tile with less precise edges. Separately, check the clip accommodates your tile thickness, as most standard clips cover roughly 3mm to 12mm.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a leveling system fix an uneven floor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. It controls lippage between adjacent tiles, not the flatness of the floor overall. If the substrate dips, clips simply hold two tiles equally out of plane. Large format tile needs flatness within about an eighth of an inch over ten feet, which requires levelling compound.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do I leave tile leveling clips in place?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Until the mortar has cured enough that the tile will not move, which is normally overnight and safer at a full 24 hours in cold or humid conditions. Removing them early lets tiles shift, and the whole point of the system is to hold the relationship while the mortar sets.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are tile leveling clips reusable?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Clips and stems are single use by design, as they are meant to snap off below the tile surface. Wedges and threaded caps are reusable, and how many cycles they survive determines your real running cost. Peygran wedges were the most durable in our testing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best system?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Rubi Delta is the best all-round choice, with the cleanest clip breaks and the most durable reusable wedges. Perfect Level Master T-Lock offers nearly the same performance at a much lower running cost, and Tuscan is the specialist pick for large format tile above 24 inches.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I really need one?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not for small format tile or mosaics, which largely self-align. The case grows with tile size and becomes compelling above about 12 inches, because large tile is more likely to be slightly bowed and any height difference between edges is far more visible on a long joint.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What clip size do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The clip width sets your grout joint, so match it to the joint you want: 2mm for rectified porcelain, 3mm or more for pressed tile with less precise edges. Separately, check the clip accommodates your tile thickness, as most standard clips cover roughly 3mm to 12mm.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can it fix an uneven floor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. It controls lippage between adjacent tiles, not the flatness of the floor overall. If the substrate dips, clips simply hold two tiles equally out of plane. Large format tile needs flatness within about an eighth of an inch over ten feet, which requires levelling compound.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do clips stay in?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Until the mortar has cured enough that the tile will not move, which is normally overnight and safer at a full 24 hours in cold or humid conditions. Removing them early lets tiles shift, and the whole point of the system is to hold the relationship while the mortar sets.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are clips reusable?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Clips and stems are single use by design, as they are meant to snap off below the tile surface. Wedges and threaded caps are reusable, and how many cycles they survive determines your real running cost. Peygran wedges were the most durable in our testing.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-24",
      dateModified: "2026-08-24",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-24",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-24",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "tile leveling system",
      "tile lippage",
      "large format tile",
      "tile installation",
      "porcelain tile",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Tile Leveling Systems</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We set 400 square feet of porcelain across seven leveling systems, measured lippage with a straightedge, and counted how many clips snapped in the wrong place. Only 7 made the cut.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#rubi-delta"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#wedge-vs-threaded"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Wedge vs Threaded Caps
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 24, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-does" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What They Actually Do</a>
          <a href="#wedge-vs-threaded" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Wedge vs Threaded Caps</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Clip Sizing and Spacing</a>
          <a href="#large-format" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Large Format Tile Rules</a>
          <a href="#thinset" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Mortar and Coverage</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Use Them</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Lippage is the difference in height between two adjacent tiles, and it is the single flaw that separates a professional tile job from an obviously amateur one. Leveling systems exist to hold neighbouring tiles flush while the mortar cures, and they work — but only if you understand what they can and cannot compensate for.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: What a Leveling System Actually Does -->
        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Leveling System Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            A tile leveling system holds the surfaces of two neighbouring tiles flush with each other while the mortar cures. That is the whole function, and understanding its limits saves a great deal of disappointment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Controls Lippage, Not Flatness</h3>

          <p>
            Lippage is a local difference in height between two adjacent tile edges. Flatness is whether the floor as a whole runs level across the room. Clips fix the first and do nothing whatsoever about the second. A floor can be perfectly free of lippage and still slope an inch across ten feet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Cannot Fix a Bad Subfloor</h3>

          <p>
            If the substrate dips, the tile follows the mortar bed, and clips will simply hold two tiles equally out of plane. Industry guidance for large format tile calls for flatness within about an eighth of an inch over ten feet, and reaching that is a levelling compound job, not a clip job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Cannot Fix a Warped Tile</h3>

          <p>
            Large porcelain tiles frequently have a slight bow through the middle. A leveling system pulls the edges flush, which is what you want, but the crown in the centre remains. On heavily warped tile the answer is a smaller offset pattern, not more clips.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Buys You Time, Which Is the Real Benefit</h3>

          <p>
            Without clips, every tile has to be tapped into plane against its neighbours while the mortar is workable, and any tile you knock while setting the next one has to be corrected immediately. Clips lock the relationship, so you can work across a floor without constantly revisiting what you have already set.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Do Not Always Need One</h3>

          <p>
            Small format tile, mosaic sheets, and anything under about eight inches generally self-aligns well enough that clips add cost and cleanup for no visible gain. The case for a leveling system grows with tile size, and becomes compelling somewhere around twelve inches.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Lippage by the Numbers</p>
