        <!-- SECTION 2: Clip and Wedge vs Threaded Cap -->
        <div id="wedge-vs-threaded" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Clip and Wedge vs Threaded Cap
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every system here belongs to one of two families, and the choice between them is about hand strength, speed, and how much control you want after the tile is down.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clip and wedge is faster.</strong> A base clip goes under the tile edge, a wedge drives in from the side, and a plier squeezes the wedge home. An experienced installer can place and tension a clip in a couple of seconds, which across a large floor is the difference between a day and a day and a half.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Threaded caps are more controllable.</strong> A stem goes under the tile and a threaded cap screws down against it. Tension is set by turning rather than squeezing, so it is repeatable, adjustable, and can be backed off if a tile needs nudging while the mortar is still open.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hand strength decides it for many people.</strong> Squeezing a plier several hundred times in a day is genuinely demanding, and anyone with a weak grip or arthritis will find threaded caps far more comfortable. This is the single most common reason people switch.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Running costs differ in where the money goes.</strong> Clip-and-wedge systems consume clips and reuse wedges. Threaded systems consume stems and reuse caps. In both cases the reusable part determines the long-run cost, so buy the system whose reusable component is well made.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Both leave a stem to remove.</strong> Neither family avoids the cleanup step. Once the mortar has set, the protruding part is knocked off along the grout line, and any that breaks badly has to be chiselled below the surface before grouting. The systems differ in how often that happens, not whether it does.
          </p>
        </div>

