        <!-- SECTION 4: Choosing Clip Size and Spacing -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing Clip Size and Spacing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying the wrong clip size is the most common ordering mistake, and it is easy to avoid once you know that the number on the packet refers to the grout joint, not the tile.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clip width equals your grout joint.</strong> A 2mm clip produces a 2mm joint, so choose the clip to match the joint you want. Rectified porcelain typically takes a joint between 2mm and 3mm; pressed tile with less precise edges needs 3mm or more to absorb the variation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the tile thickness range separately.</strong> Every clip has a range of tile thicknesses it accommodates, commonly around 3mm to 12mm. Thick porcelain pavers and gauged panels sit outside standard ranges and need clips specified for them, so read the packet rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Spacing is roughly every eight to twelve inches along a joint.</strong> A 12 inch tile typically wants one clip per edge, a 24 inch tile two, and a long plank tile three. The goal is that no unsupported span between clips is long enough for the edge to rise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Corners need a clip, not four.</strong> At a four-way intersection a single clip placed across the joint controls the relationship between all the tiles meeting there. Stacking clips at corners wastes consumables and makes the cleanup worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Order about twenty percent more than you calculate.</strong> Clips are lost, broken during placement, and used in places you did not plan. Running out mid-floor with mortar open is a far more expensive problem than a leftover bag.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Do the Clip Maths Before You Buy Tile</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Take your square footage, work out how many tiles that is, and multiply by the number of clips each tile needs on its unsupported edges. A 200 square foot floor in 12 inch tile needs roughly 400 clips. People routinely buy a bag of 100 and discover the problem halfway across the room.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Large Format Tile and Offset Rules -->
        <div id="large-format" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Large Format Tile and Offset Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            Large format tile is where leveling systems stop being a convenience and become close to mandatory, and it also comes with a rule that surprises most people the first time they hear it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never run a fifty percent offset on large tile.</strong> The industry guidance limits offset to about a third of the tile length for anything over 15 inches. A running bond at half offset places the centre of one tile — the highest point of any bow — directly against the edge of its neighbour, which guarantees visible lippage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Warpage is normal, not a defect.</strong> Large porcelain is fired at high temperature and comes out with a slight crown. Manufacturers allow for it within a tolerance, so returning tile for it rarely goes anywhere. Designing the layout around it is the practical response.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Back-buttering stops being optional.</strong> Large tile needs mortar on the tile as well as the substrate to reach the coverage the manufacturer requires, and voids under a large tile are where cracks start. Clips hold the surface flush but cannot support an unsupported area.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a bigger trowel than feels reasonable.</strong> Large format tile typically calls for a half inch square notch or larger, and medium bed mortar rather than standard thinset. Trying to set a 24 inch tile with a quarter inch trowel leaves voids no clip can fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Two people is the honest requirement.</strong> Gauged porcelain panels are heavy, flexible, and unforgiving of being set at an angle. Attempting large panels alone is how they crack before they are even down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Why Mortar Matters More Than Clips -->
        <div id="thinset" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Mortar Matters More Than Clips
          </h2>

          <p>
            A leveling system is a finishing aid, not a substitute for setting the tile properly. Most floors that fail visually fail because of what is under the tile, not what was clipped to its edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Coverage is the number that matters.</strong> Standard guidance asks for around eighty percent mortar coverage on a dry interior floor and ninety-five percent in a wet area or exterior installation. Pull a set tile up early in the job and look at the back to check you are actually achieving it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Comb the mortar in one direction.</strong> Swirled trowel patterns trap air and reduce coverage. Straight parallel ridges collapse into each other and push air out to the edge, which is why every manufacturer specifies them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not let the mortar skin over.</strong> Thinset that has begun to crust will not bond, and it looks perfectly fine when you set the tile onto it. Comb out only as much as you can cover in a few minutes, and touch it before setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Medium bed mortar for large tile.</strong> Standard thinset is formulated for a thin bed and shrinks as it cures, which is why a thick application under a large tile pulls unevenly. Medium bed products are designed for the thicker beds large format tile requires.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clips fight against poor coverage and lose.</strong> A tile bedded on high spots of mortar will settle after the clip comes off, and a tile sitting on a void will flex. Neither is a leveling system problem, but both get blamed on one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Using a Leveling System Correctly -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Using a Leveling System Correctly
          </h2>

          <p>
            The technique is simple and the mistakes are consistent, which means a short list covers almost everything that goes wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Slide the clip under the tile, do not push it in after.</strong> The base plate needs to be bedded in mortar under the tile edge, which means placing it before or as the tile goes down. Forcing a clip under a set tile lifts the edge and defeats the point.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tension the wedge firmly, then stop.</strong> The tiles should pull flush and the wedge should be tight. Squeezing harder past that point does nothing useful and is the main cause of clips snapping in place while you work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check with a straightedge as you go.</strong> Clips control neighbouring tiles relative to each other, which means a drift can accumulate across a floor without any single joint looking wrong. A four foot level laid across every few rows catches it early.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wait the full cure before removing anything.</strong> Knocking clips off too early moves the tile. Overnight is usually the minimum and a full day is safer in cold or humid conditions. If the mortar bag says otherwise, believe the bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Kick along the joint, not across it.</strong> Striking the clip in line with the grout line snaps it at the base. Striking across the joint levers the tile edge upward and is how people crack a tile after successfully setting it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clear every stub before grouting.</strong> Any remnant sitting proud of the mortar bed will telegraph through the grout or prevent it filling the joint properly. A quick pass with a chisel or oscillating tool along each joint is worth the twenty minutes.
          </p>
        </div>

