              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What clip size do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The clip width sets your grout joint, so match it to the joint you want: 2mm for rectified porcelain, 3mm or more for pressed tile with less precise edges. Separately, check the clip accommodates your tile thickness, as most standard clips cover roughly 3mm to 12mm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can it fix an uneven floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. It controls lippage between adjacent tiles, not the flatness of the floor overall. If the substrate dips, clips simply hold two tiles equally out of plane. Large format tile needs flatness within about an eighth of an inch over ten feet, which requires levelling compound.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do clips stay in?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Until the mortar has cured enough that the tile will not move, which is normally overnight and safer at a full 24 hours in cold or humid conditions. Removing them early lets tiles shift, and the whole point of the system is to hold the relationship while the mortar sets.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are clips reusable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clips and stems are single use by design, as they are meant to snap off below the tile surface. Wedges and threaded caps are reusable, and how many cycles they survive determines your real running cost. Peygran wedges were the most durable in our testing.
              </p>
