        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Tile Leveling Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Leveling system errors tend to be procedural rather than product related. The system usually works; the way it was used did not.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Clips to Fix the Subfloor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A leveling system holds adjacent tiles flush with each other, which is a completely different thing from making the floor flat. If the substrate dips, the tile follows it and the clips hold two tiles equally out of plane. Check flatness with a straightedge and fix it with levelling compound before any tile goes down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a 50 Percent Offset on Large Tile</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A half offset places the crown at the centre of one tile against the edge of the next, which produces visible lippage no clip can pull out. Industry guidance caps offset at about a third of the tile length above 15 inches. Design the layout for the tile you actually bought.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing the Clips Too Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Knocking clips off before the mortar has cured lets the tile move, which undoes the entire job silently. Overnight is the minimum, and a full day is sensible in cold or damp conditions. The temptation to check progress the same evening has cost more than one floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Striking Across the Grout Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clip snaps cleanly when struck along the joint and levers the tile edge upward when struck across it. Cracking a tile during cleanup, after successfully setting the entire floor, is a particularly frustrating way to lose a tile and it happens constantly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

